WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.918
Interactions are useful when they make learners think, decide, compare, practice, or apply.

2
00:00:05.918 --> 00:00:15.282
Click-to-reveal elements, tabs, markers, accordions, process interactions, and branching scenarios can break content into manageable pieces.

3
00:00:15.282 --> 00:00:19.178
They should not be added only because a screen looks too quiet.

4
00:00:19.178 --> 00:00:23.822
Extra clicks without purpose can create friction instead of engagement.

5
00:00:23.822 --> 00:00:29.291
A simple interaction can work well when the objective is exploration or organization.

6
00:00:29.291 --> 00:00:33.486
For example, tabs may separate roles, phases, risks, or examples.

7
00:00:33.486 --> 00:00:37.456
Markers can call attention to parts of an interface or diagram.

8
00:00:37.456 --> 00:00:40.078
A process interaction can show sequence.

9
00:00:40.078 --> 00:00:48.319
These patterns are effective when the learner needs to understand relationships, not when the designer simply wants to hide text.

10
00:00:48.319 --> 00:00:52.364
Scenarios are powerful because they place learning in context.

11
00:00:52.364 --> 00:00:59.106
A decision-based scenario asks learners to choose an action, see feedback, and understand consequences.

12
00:00:59.106 --> 00:01:02.477
The scenario does not need to be dramatic to be useful.

13
00:01:02.477 --> 00:01:08.770
It should reflect realistic decisions, common mistakes, and the judgment learners need on the job.

14
00:01:08.770 --> 00:01:14.463
Feedback should explain why an answer is strong or weak, not just say correct or incorrect.

15
00:01:14.463 --> 00:01:20.306
Software demonstrations and simulations can help when learners need procedural practice.

16
00:01:20.306 --> 00:01:24.576
Keep them focused on the task, not every feature in the application.

17
00:01:24.576 --> 00:01:27.348
Align every interaction with an objective.

18
00:01:27.348 --> 00:01:31.168
If an interaction does not support the outcome, simplify it.

19
00:01:31.168 --> 00:01:39.634
The best practice activity feels like a rehearsal for real work rather than an obstacle placed between the learner and the next screen.

20
00:01:39.634 --> 00:01:44.503
When designing practice, begin with the mistake learners are likely to make.

21
00:01:44.503 --> 00:01:49.522
Then create an interaction that helps them recognize and correct that mistake.

22
00:01:49.522 --> 00:01:54.167
This keeps the activity grounded in real performance instead of novelty.

23
00:01:54.167 --> 00:02:00.909
A small, well-targeted decision can teach more than a complex interaction that does not connect to the job.
